{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Tx9v1zPayzgof2QyP5KDRa","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Conventions\n2\t\n3\tThe standards the code of 495 meets. `AGENTS.md` (symlinked as `CLAUDE.md`) carries the project\n4\tspine — stack, commands, architecture, never-do list. How a change is made — the six steps, the\n5\treview, the acceptance run, the landing, the commit messages — is `cycle/README.md`.\n6\t\n7\t## Preflight\n8\t\n9\t**Preflight** is `npm run check`: typecheck, the test suite and the `lint:*` scripts chained\n10\ttogether, under Node 24, the declared floor (`specs/adr/D-50`). It is green before any forward work,\n11\tnot \"green enough for this task\". A defect costs roughly 1× to fix in development, 10× in\n12\tintegration, 100× in production: fix a red gate now.\n13\t\n14\t| Control | Refuses |\n15\t|---------|---------|\n16\t| `lint:code` | what Biome refuses (`specs/adr/D-42`, `D-43`) |\n17\t| `lint:layers` | an import against the direction of `AGENTS.md` § Architecture |\n18\t| `lint:architecture` | a `CMP-*` id claimed in `src/` without a row in `specs/archive/amont/conception-technique.md` §4.1, or an import cycle |\n19\t| `lint:exports` | an export nothing reads outside its module (`specs/adr/D-44`) |\n20\t| `lint:traceability` | a `[P0]` requirement of `specs/archive/amont/expression-besoins.md` absent from `specs/archive/TRACEABILITY.md` |\n21\t| `lint:declarations`, `lint:distribution` | a published surface that drifts from `src/` |\n22\t| `lint:story-format` | a story of `specs/stories/` that departs from `cycle/format-de-story.md` |\n23\t\n24\t## Code Style\n25\t\n26\t- One thing per function, one responsibility per module (SRP).\n27\t- Prefer small, focused modules over god files. Split a file when it grows a second responsibility.\n28\t- Give every name a specific, unique meaning. Avoid `data`, `handler`, `Manager`, `Service`.\n29\t- Type everything explicitly. Never use `any`. Never leave a public function untyped.\n30\t- Extract shared logic into one function or module. Never duplicate logic.\n31\t- Prefer an early return over a nested `if`.\n32\t- Throw an exception instead of returning an error code or a boolean sentinel.\n33\t- Delete dead code. Never comment it out — git history already holds it.\n34\t- Boy Scout Rule: leave every file you touch at least as clean as you found it.\n35\t- Write the minimum code that solves the stated problem. No preventive abstraction, no unused\n36\t  configuration layer. Inspect what the dependencies already do before adding a package or writing\n37\t  a capability a maintained library provides.\n38\t\n39\t## Comments\n40\t\n41\t- Keep existing comments on a refactor. They carry intent and provenance.\n42\t- Write why, never what. The code already says what it does.\n43\t- Never write an obvious comment that restates the code.\n44\t- Never leave commented-out code. Delete it and rely on git history.\n45\t- Describe the behavior of the code, never the process that produced it: no ticket, story, review\n46\t  round or session reference in a comment.\n47\t\n48\t## Tests (F.I.R.S.T)\n49\t\n50\t- Run the whole suite headless with one command: `npm test`.\n51\t- Give every new function a test. Give every bug fix a regression test.\n52\t- Name a fake used for external I/O as a fake class, not an inline stub.\n53\t- Keep every test Fast, Independent, Repeatable, Self-Validating, and Timely.\n54\t- Never skip or ignore a test without a written note on what stays unresolved.\n55\t- Test every boundary condition: empty input, maximum, minimum, and the off-by-one case.\n56\t- Assert only through the public interface — return values, contracts, view state. Never assert on\n57\t  private state.\n58\t- A test is written before the code it pins, and fails on the assertion the story states before\n59\t  that code exists (`cycle/README.md` § Les six pas).\n60\t\n61\t## Dependencies\n62\t\n63\t- Inject a dependency through a constructor or parameter. Never reach for a global or a bare import.\n64\t- Wrap a third-party library behind a project-owned port (see `src/ports/`).\n65\t- Keep a dependency at its latest published version whenever Preflight stays green; a caret on a\n66\t  `0.x` version silently locks the minor. `@types/node` tracks `engines.node`, not the newest\n67\t  release (`specs/adr/D-50`).\n68\t\n69\t## Pi is the host, not one dependency among others\n70\t\n71\t495 is an extension of Pi. Pi is what keeps 495 from rebuilding what already exists, and what lets\n72\tit state a fact instead of inferring one. Reach for Pi's own API first — before writing the\n73\tcapability, and before deducing from the outside what Pi can report from the inside\n74\t(`specs/adr/D-55`).\n75\t\n76\t- **Look for the API before building.** A hook, an event, a runtime or a typed result Pi already\n77\t  publishes beats a table 495 maintains, a file 495 parses, or a fact 495 reads out of a package's\n78\t  code.\n79\t- **Read the documentation the pinned package ships.** It is already on disk, at the exact version\n80\t  installed: `node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/docs/`, and `extensions.md` there carries\n81\t  the event list. Prefer it to any URL: <https://pi.dev/docs/latest> tracks the newest Pi, not the\n82\t  one this repository has.\n83\t- **Confirm in the pinned surface before building on it.** `@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/dist/**/*.d.ts`\n84\t  and the peer packages say what the release ships. What is built on an API names the version it\n85\t  was confirmed against.\n86\t- **Prefer an observed fact to a restated one.** What Pi hands over was measured; what 495 restates\n87\t  is believed, and drifts the day the host changes. Say in the code which of the two a value is.\n88\t- **A workaround names what it replaces.** When no API covers the need, write where the workaround\n89\t  lives what was looked for and not found, so it can be deleted the day it arrives.\n90\t- **Leaning on Pi never 